1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:15,616
A model can score well on general Chinese benchmarks and still misread an idiom, a classical
word, or a Cantonese aspect marker. ChAAt exposes these hidden language-specific gaps.

2
00:00:16,816 --> 00:00:26,601
This is an English-language demo mode. It loads six models, fifty hard items, and three
repeated runs without using an API key or searching the web.

3
00:00:27,801 --> 00:00:44,501
The overview ranks models from one hundred to thirty-one percent. The heatmap shows where
capability breaks down. GPT-4o mini reaches only twenty-eight percent on idioms and thirteen
percent on classical Chinese, while DeepSeek Flash is at one hundred percent.

4
00:00:47,510 --> 00:00:53,083
ChAAt does not stop at a score. It retrieves the exact failed item.

5
00:00:54,283 --> 00:01:08,112
GPT-4o mini interprets ceng ji he shi as a long time ago, but the idiom means not long ago.
The system shows the expected answer, the model output, and a source-grounded explanation.

6
00:01:09,312 --> 00:01:24,789
The evaluation has three tiers: fifty diagnostic items, forty-one exam-style items from
public mock exams, and six open-ended tasks. Answer positions are balanced, scoring is
reproducible, and external search is disabled.

7
00:01:25,989 --> 00:01:40,190
For open-ended answers, two raters scored every response, and a blind third rater scored all
thirty-six. Three-rater reliability was zero point nine six. This separates model
performance from scoring artifacts.

8
00:01:41,390 --> 00:01:55,856
ChAAt helps researchers compare Chinese-specific capabilities, locate failure cases, and
select models for real applications. The system is reproducible, extensible, and designed
for both evaluation and linguistic diagnosis.
